From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] nfc: s3fwrn5: apply the RF configuration without a firmware
 update

The controller on this phone enumerates, reports its protocols and accepts
RF_DISCOVER with status 0, and then finds nothing. Its RF registers have
never been programmed.

s3fwrn5_nci_rf_configure() is the only thing in this driver that programs
them, and it is reachable from exactly one place: after a firmware download
inside s3fwrn5_firmware_update(). If the firmware image is absent,
post_setup returns early; if it is present but the chip is already at that
version, firmware_update takes its `goto out` and skips the RF
configuration as well. Either way a chip with current firmware and blank RF
registers stays blank.

That is fine on a phone whose vendor stack configures RF from userspace,
which is what Android does here -- android.hardware.nfc-service.sec plus
libnfc-sec-vendor.conf, which names the blobs and the clock parameters. It
is not fine when the kernel is the only thing driving the part.

So try the RF configuration on its own when there is no firmware image. It
costs one request_firmware() that fails harmlessly when the file is absent,
which is the situation on every board that does not ship one, and the log
line says plainly that the chip was left as it was.

This does not touch the firmware download path, deliberately. Writing the
wrong image to an NFC controller is the one failure here that is not
recoverable in software, and programming RF registers is not.

Worth recording for whoever takes this further: the part is an S3NRN4V, not
an S3FWRN5, and its blobs are sec_s3nrn4v_firmware.bin plus *two* register
files, sec_s3nrn4v_hwreg.bin and sec_s3nrn4v_swreg.bin, where this driver
knows one rfreg. The firmware container is the same -- a 12 byte date, the
version at 0x10, signature and image offsets after it, all parsing cleanly
against S3FWRN5_FW_IMAGE_HEADER_SIZE. And the vendor configures the clock
with FW_CFG_CLK_SPEED=0x11 where this driver hardcodes clk_speed = 0xff,
which is the next thing to look at if the registers go in and nothing
changes.

Two things were tried here and removed, both recorded so nobody adds them
back. Applying a second register file, which the vendor stack does -- its
config names RF_HW_FILE_NAME and RF_SW_FILE_NAME -- makes the chip stop
answering PROP_STOP_RFREG, and the same happens with the two files
concatenated into one image. And calling nci_core_reset() afterwards, which
the firmware-update path does, is not safe from post_setup(): it runs
inside nci_open_device()'s own init sequence, and re-entering the request
machinery there returns -EPROTO and leaves the adapter refusing to power on
at all.

--- a/drivers/nfc/s3fwrn5/core.c
+++ b/drivers/nfc/s3fwrn5/core.c
@@ -128,7 +128,29 @@
 	int ret;
 
 	if (s3fwrn5_firmware_init(info)) {
-		//skip bootloader mode
+		/*
+		 * No firmware image. The chip keeps its own, so this is not
+		 * fatal -- but the RF registers still have to be programmed, and
+		 * the only path that did that was the one this skips. Try it on
+		 * its own before giving up.
+		 *
+		 * Deliberately nothing else. An earlier version of this also
+		 * called nci_core_reset() here, on the reasoning that the
+		 * firmware-update path ends that way and transferring registers
+		 * is not the same as running on them. It is not safe from here:
+		 * post_setup() is called from inside nci_open_device()'s own
+		 * init sequence, and re-entering the request machinery from
+		 * there fails -- observed as -EPROTO, with the adapter then
+		 * refusing to power on at all, even with no rfreg file present.
+		 */
+		s3fwrn5_set_mode(info, S3FWRN5_MODE_NCI);
+		s3fwrn5_set_wake(info, true);
+		ret = s3fwrn5_nci_rf_configure(info, "sec_s3fwrn5_rfreg.bin");
+		s3fwrn5_set_wake(info, false);
+		if (ret < 0)
+			dev_info(&ndev->nfc_dev->dev,
+				 "no rfreg applied (%d); the chip keeps whatever it has\n",
+				 ret);
 		return 0;
 	}
 
